The Security Council,

Recalling its resolutions 2042 (2012), 2043 (2012), 2118 (2013), 2139 (2014), 2165 (2014), 2175 (2014), 2191 (2014), 2209 (2015), 2254 (2015), 2258 (2015), 2268 (2016), 2286 (2016), 2332 (2016), 2336 (2016), 2393 (2017), 2401 (2018), and 2449 (2018), and the relevant statements of its President,

Reaffirming its strong commitment to the sovereignty, independence, unity and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic, and to the purposes and principles of the Charter of the United Nations,

Expressing grave concern over the ongoing humanitarian situation in the Syrian Arab Republic, particularly in north-western Syria, and emphasizing that the humanitarian situation can only be alleviated through a comprehensive and sustainable political settlement,

Welcoming the announcement of a cessation of hostilities in the Idlib de-escalation area that took effect on 31 August 2019, and emphasizing the necessity of preserving and consolidating this arrangement,

Expressing deep concern at the continued presence and violent activities of terrorist groups in north-western Syria, in particular Hayat Tahrir al-Sham (formerly Jabhat al-Nusra), Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL/Da'esh), and other individuals, groups, undertakings and entities associated with Al-Qaida, as designated by the Security Council, which continue to threaten the security of civilians and launch attacks against residential areas and civilian infrastructure,

Reaffirming that Member States must ensure that any measures taken to combat terrorism comply with all their obligations under international law, in particular international human rights law, refugee law and international humanitarian law,

Reiterating that the only sustainable solution to the Syrian crisis is through an inclusive and Syrian-led, Syrian-owned political process facilitated by the United Nations, in full accordance with resolution 2254 (2015) and the 2012 Geneva Communiqué,

1. Demands that all parties firmly respect and implement the cessation of hostilities in the Idlib de-escalation area;

2. Stresses that the cessation of hostilities shall not apply to military operations against individuals, groups, undertakings and entities associated with the Islamic State in Iraq and the Levant (ISIL/Da'esh), Al-Qaida and Al-Nusra Front, and all other individuals, groups, undertakings and entities associated with Al-Qaida or ISIL, and other terrorist groups, as designated by the Security Council;

3. Calls upon all parties to ensure rapid, safe and unhindered humanitarian access to all people in need throughout the Syrian Arab Republic, in accordance with the guiding principles of United Nations humanitarian assistance set out in General Assembly resolution 46/182, and with full respect for the sovereignty and territorial integrity of the Syrian Arab Republic;

4. Condemns the use of civilians as human shields by terrorist groups and demands that designated terrorist organizations cease all attacks on civilian populations and infrastructure;

5. Urges all parties to protect civilians and civilian objects in accordance with international humanitarian law;

6. Welcomes the significant progress achieved toward the establishment and early convening of a credible, balanced and inclusive Syrian-led and Syrian-owned Constitutional Committee under the auspices of the United Nations in Geneva, and calls on all parties to constructively engage to advance the political process in line with resolution 2254 (2015);

7. Decides to remain actively seized of the matter.

